# Assumptions and verification

Collection dates are interpreted as DD/MM/YYYY and stored as ISO dates. Alfa (2026-02-10) is the first report; Beta was collected on 2026-08-18 (the issue date is not used). Equivalent marker names are merged. The first report uses mg/dL for glucose, LDL and creatinine, and % for HbA1c.

Conversions: glucose mmol/L multiplied by 18.018; LDL mmol/L multiplied by 38.67; creatinine micromol/L divided by 88.4; HbA1c IFCC mmol/mol converted to NGSP percent as 0.09148 * value + 2.152 (https://ngsp.org/ifccngsp.asp). Converted results are rounded to two decimals for storage; classification uses unrounded values.

Worse means the latest value is further outside the first report's reference interval than the initial value. First intervals: glucose 70–99 mg/dL, LDL strictly below 130 mg/dL, creatinine 0.70–1.30 mg/dL, HbA1c 4.0–5.6%. This is a reference-range comparison, not a clinical diagnosis.

All eight results and both collection dates were read from the two PDFs using pdftotext. Glucose rises from 92 to 109.91 mg/dL; LDL rises from 118 to 143.08 mg/dL: both now exceed the first limits. Creatinine becomes 0.86 mg/dL and HbA1c 5.54%, both within the first intervals. HbA1c rises slightly but does not qualify for worse. JSON was loaded back and checked; unrounded conversion/reference comparisons were asserted successfully.


